[[File:6n-graf.svg|right|thumb|A graph, such as is the subject of '''graph theory''']]

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable|mathematics}} The branch of [[mathematics]] dealing with the properties of [[graph]]s (networks of [[vertex|vertices]] and [[edge]]s).
#: {{ux|en|The type of graph studied in '''graph theory''' is formally described as an [[ordered pair]] <math>G=(V, E)</math> comprising a set <math>V</math> of [[vertex|vertices]], or [[node]]s, and a set <math>E</math> of pairs (either ordered or unordered) of vertices, each representing an (directed or undirected) [[edge]]; if desired, the vertices and/or edges can be labelled.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1993|author=Richard J. Trudeau|title=Introduction to '''Graph Theory'''|publisher=Dover|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=eRLEAgAAQBAJ&printsec=frontcover&dq=%22graph+theory%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjwgNrB98nsAhXqzjgGHdf9CgcQ6AEwBHoECCYQAg#v=onepage&q=%22graph%20theory%22&f=false|page=47
|passage=In '''graph theory''' there are two concepts of "sameness" whereby graphs are judged to be "the same", namely equality and [[isomorphism]].{{...}}An indication of the pervasive role isomorphism has in '''graph theory''' is the fact that isomorphism has virtually captured the word "is".}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1998|author=Béla Bollobás|title=Modern '''Graph Theory'''|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=JeIlBQAAQBAJ&printsec=frontcover&dq=%22graph+theory%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjwgNrB98nsAhXqzjgGHdf9CgcQ6AEwAnoECAUQAg#v=onepage&q=%22graph%20theory%22&f=false|page=103|publisher=Springer
|passage=Extremal problems are at the very heart of '''graph theory'''. Interpreting it broadly, extremal '''graph theory''' encompasses most of '''graph theory''', in its narrow sense, it contains many of the deepest and most beautiful results in '''graph theory'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|author=Edmund Burke; Dominique de Werra; Jeffrey Kingston|chapter=5.6: Applications to Timetabling|editors=Jonathan L. Gross; Jay Yellen|title=Handbook of Graph Theory|publisher=CRC Press|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=mKkIGIea_BkC&printsec=frontcover&dq=%22graph+theory%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjwgNrB98nsAhXqzjgGHdf9CgcQ6AEwAXoECAEQAg#v=onepage&q=%22graph%20theory%22&f=false|page=445
|passage=We aim to highlight the role of '''graph theory''' in modern timetabling research and provide some pointers to the relevant literature for the interested reader.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|applied mathematics}} A [[theory]] developed by applying graph theory to a particular problem or application.
#* '''2009''', Fu Lee Wang, Christopher C. Yang, ''Chapter XI: Extracting the Essence: Automatic Text Summarization'', Yin-Leng Theng, Schubert Foo, Dion Goh, Jin-Cheon Na (editors), ''Handbook of Research on Digital Libraries'', IGI Global (Information Science Reference), [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=M6CB7Uhx1zgC&pg=PA114&dq=%22graph+theories%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjoxcGA_8TsAhVWyDgGHQRBA5oQ6AEwEHoECDkQAg#v=onepage&q=%22graph%20theories%22&f=false page 114],
#*: The entity-level approaches build an internal representation for text units and their relationships, and use '''graph theories''' to determine the significance of units.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=Tony D. Sampson|chapter=6: Contagion Theory: Beyond the Microbe|editors=Arthur Kroker; Marilouise Kroker|title=Critical Digital Studies: A Reader|edition=2nd|publisher=w:University of Toronto Press|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=NWZkAgAAQBAJ&pg=PA131&dq=%22graph+theories%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjoxcGA_8TsAhVWyDgGHQRBA5oQ6AEwDHoECDEQAg#v=onepage&q=%22graph%20theories%22&f=false|page=131
|passage=Galloway and Thacker's own dissatisfaction with the '''graph theories''' of network science, for example, point to a tendency to attribute unfettered and apolitical naturalness to what are in effect asymmetrical topological spaces.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014|author=Matteo Fumagalli|title=Increasing Perceptual Skills of Robots Through Proximal Force/Torque Sensors|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=N0S9BAAAQBAJ&pg=PA46&dq=%22graph+theories%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjoxcGA_8TsAhVWyDgGHQRBA5oQ6AEwCnoECDAQAg#v=onepage&q=%22graph%20theories%22&f=false|page=46|publisher=Springer
|passage=Chapter 3 has shown a method that makes use of a graphical formulation employing '''graph theories''' for performing the computation of both kinematic quantities (i.e. angular velocities of the center of mass of links, but also linear and angular acceleration), and dynamic (internal forces on the connection elements between the links, but also externally applied wrenches).}}

